Acute Otitis Externa
Common Infections and Afflictions
Tragal tenderness, Ear canal erythema and edema, Otorrhea

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-001
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting unilateral ear pain and purulent discharge.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting severe, rapidly escalating ear pain over the past 8 hours, uncontrolled by maximum doses of over-the-counter analgesia, preventing sleep and causing significant distress. Given the aggressive trajectory of pain, signs of potential localized extension, and extreme discomfort, immediate on-site assessment is required to provide targeted analgesia, initiate topical antimicrobial therapy, and exclude perichondritis or malignant otitis externa. Delaying assessment and intervention until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of prolonged suffering and localized complication, satisfying criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: The pain is sharp and throbbing, and my ear feels completely blocked.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced approximately 48 hours prior with mild pruritus of the right ear canal, progressing to a steady feeling of fullness and pressure. Over the last 12 hours, pain has escalated severely, particularly exacerbated by jaw movement or incidental contact with the outer ear. Patient notes a thick purulent, yellowish otorrhea and subjective conductive hearing loss. Tragal tenderness and pain with pinna traction are heavily reported. Denies systemic toxicity, chills, or subjective fevers.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Solitary adult. Acutely distressed due to sleep deprivation secondary to severe localized pain. No immediate physical safety hazards identified.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, acutely distressed due to localized pain. Guarding the right side of the head.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Right ear reveals extreme tragal tenderness and severe pain upon pinna traction. External auditory canal is markedly erythematous and edematous, restricting full visibility of the tympanic membrane. Moderate amounts of purulent, serous otorrhea present in the meatus. Mild preauricular regional lymphadenopathy noted. Left ear canal and tympanic membrane unremarkable. Cranial nerves intact. No facial asymmetry.
Environmental Observations: Home environment secure, appropriate temperature control, unobstructed access to bathroom and kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescribed medications. Paracetamol 1000mg taken 4-hourly over the last 12 hours, total 3g ingested; minimal analgesic effect.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Acute Otitis Externa - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out due to absence of typical prodromal upper respiratory symptoms and the presence of extreme pain with pinna manipulation, which is not characteristic of primary acute otitis media.
Malignant Otitis Externa - Unlikely due to the absence of severe systemic signs, lack of significant immunosuppressive history (e.g., poorly controlled diabetes mellitus), and absence of frank granulation tissue on the visible meatus.
Contact Dermatitis of Ear Canal - Ruled out due to the lack of known topical exposure, presence of significant purulent discharge rather than isolated scaling or pruritus, and the extreme degree of acute pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
Telephone triage identified a patient in extreme, worsening pain localized to the right ear, completely refractory to maximal over-the-counter analgesia. The severity of the pain and the rapidly deteriorating clinical picture necessitated an urgent home assessment to differentiate standard acute otitis externa from more sinister, deeply invasive complications like malignant otitis externa or perichondritis, which require immediate hospital-level intervention. Clinical findings confirm a severe, localized external canal infection. The immediate provision of prescription-strength topical antibiotic and corticosteroid drops, alongside tailored systemic analgesia, definitively could not be safely delayed until the next available in-hours period, rigorously fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ciprofloxacin 0.3% / Hydrocortisone 1% ear drops, 3 drops to the affected ear twice daily for 7 days. Advised Ibuprofen 400mg every 8 hours as needed for pain, alongside continued Paracetamol. Ear canal swab for bacterial and fungal culture is indicated if refractory to initial 48 hours of targeted therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce inflammation rapidly, instill drops consistently on a strict schedule. Place the medication bottle on the bedside table as a definitive visual cue. Do not insert cotton buds; avoiding canal trauma facilitates uninterrupted epithelial healing and prevents mechanical bacterial re-introduction.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 48-72 hours if no symptomatic improvement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present directly to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing spreading redness or swelling behind the ear, high-grade fever, facial weakness, or focal neurological deficits.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending clinical response to topical therapy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expected to improve within 48 to 72 hours of initiating topical therapy. Pain should recede as local edema resolves. Complete resolution typically requires 7 to 10 days of continuous and compliant treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if pain becomes completely unmanageable, if facial asymmetry occurs, or if swelling appears directly behind the ear.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The prescribed drops combine an antibiotic to eradicate the bacterial overgrowth and a steroid to rapidly decrease localized swelling and pain within the canal.
Actionable Steps: Lie on the unaffected side for 5 minutes after instilling drops to ensure deep penetration. Keep the ear completely dry during showering by using a cotton ball heavily coated in petroleum jelly placed at the outer meatus.

